//! Mutexes, condition variables and channels between threads.
/* std/sync: the mutex surface (B-5). `Mutex<T>` itself is a compiler builtin,
   in the shape `Atomic<T>`/`Thread<T>` have: a monomorphized per-element-type
   representation, not `Copy`, with its method set lowered to the C runtime's
   pthread mutex rather than written in Kelp. Its surface is

       fun Mutex.new(value: move T) => Mutex<T>
       fun Mutex<T>.lock(block: scoped fun(mut T) => R) => R
       fun Mutex<T>.into_inner() => T

   `Mutex.new` takes the guarded value by move. `lock` runs a scoped block while
   the mutex is held: the block receives `v: mut T`, a mutable borrow that is
   valid only inside the block (the existing scoped-block rules refuse storing
   or returning it), the block's value is returned, and the mutex is released on
   every exit of the block. A panic inside the block releases it too and marks
   the mutex POISONED, so a later `lock` panics with "mutex poisoned"; there is
   no unwinding, so the panicking thread's frame is abandoned
   (`plan/mk9-memory-model.md` section 2). `into_inner` consumes the mutex and
   returns the value it held.

   `Mutex<T>` is `Sync`: a shared borrow of it may cross a thread boundary when
   `T` is `Send`-shaped, exactly as a `&Atomic<T>` may. It is hosted only: a
   freestanding manifest refuses it, because the runtime half is a pthread mutex
   (a future spin-lock option is what a kernel would use instead).

   The two calls have no `.kp` declarations for the reason `std/atomic`
   documents the atomic methods it declares nowhere: the compiler builds their
   signatures at the call site and the C runtime lowers them. This module exists
   so a program can name the surface it is using (`impl "std/sync"`), and so the
   module list in `plan/` has the one id the mutex belongs to.

   The rest of the family lives here too, all builtin in the same shape:

       fun RwLock.new(value: move T) => RwLock<T>
       fun RwLock<T>.read(block: scoped fun(&T) => R) => R
       fun RwLock<T>.write(block: scoped fun(mut T) => R) => R
       fun RwLock<T>.try_read(block: scoped fun(&T) => R) => Option<R>
       fun RwLock<T>.try_write(block: scoped fun(mut T) => R) => Option<R>
       fun RwLock<T>.into_inner() => T

   `RwLock<T>` is writer-preferring: many readers at once, one writer alone,
   and a waiting writer blocks new readers so a stream of readers cannot starve
   it. A panic inside `write` poisons it; a panic inside `read` releases the
   read without poisoning, because a reader never writes the value. The `try_*`
   forms run their block only when the lock was free and hand the value back in
   `Option` (`None` when it was busy).

       fun Condvar.new() => Condvar
       fun Condvar.notify_one()
       fun Condvar.notify_all()
       fun Mutex<T>.wait_while(cv: &Condvar, pred: fun(&T) => bool, block: scoped fun(mut T) => R) => R
       fun Mutex<T>.wait_while_timeout(cv: &Condvar, pred: fun(&T) => bool, millis: i64, block: scoped fun(mut T) => R) => Option<R>

   A wait has to release the mutex's own lock atomically around the sleep,
   which a scoped guard cannot express, so the wait lives on the mutex:
   `m.wait_while(cv, pred) { v -> ... }` re-tests `pred(&v)` after every wakeup
   (spurious wakeups are the library's problem) and then runs the block with
   the lock held. There is no bare `wait`. The timed form is `None` when the
   deadline passed with the predicate still true, `Some` otherwise.

   `m.share()` and `cv.share()` return another owned handle to the same
   runtime record (a reference count; the last handle's drop frees it), and
   `m.swap(value)` exchanges the guarded value in one locked step. Channels,
   below, and `std/pool` are built from them.

   Every record here is a pthread object, so a freestanding manifest refuses
   each by name (`kelp wiki page stdlib/sync.md`). */

/* Channels, in pure Kelp over the builtins above (`kelp wiki page stdlib/sync.md` section 3,
   `kelp wiki page stdlib/sync.md` for the shape that was chosen):

       fun sync.bounded<T>(cap: usize) => (Sender<T>, Receiver<T>)
       fun sync.unbounded<T>() => (Sender<T>, Receiver<T>)
       fun sync.rendezvous<T>() => (Sender<T>, Receiver<T>)
       fun Sender<T>.send(value: move T) => option.Option<T>
       fun Sender<T>.clone() => Sender<T>
       fun Receiver<T>.recv() => result.Result<T, RecvErr>
       fun Receiver<T>.try_recv() => option.Option<T>
       fun Receiver<T>.recv_timeout(millis: i64) => result.Result<T, RecvTimeout>
       fun sync.select2<T>(a: &Receiver<T>, b: &Receiver<T>) => Pick<T>
       fun sync.select3<T>(a: &Receiver<T>, b: &Receiver<T>, c: &Receiver<T>) => Pick<T>
       for v in rx { ... }

   `rendezvous` is the zero-capacity form: one value is in flight at a time and
   `send` returns only after a receive has taken that value (`None` then), so a
   send and a receive meet. `bounded(0)` stays a panic; the rendezvous is its own
   constructor so the panic message and its fixture keep their meaning.

   `select2` and `select3` take receive cases over one element type and return
   the first case, in argument order, that is ready: `Pick.Got(index, value)` for
   a value, `Pick.Closed(index)` for a channel whose senders are all gone and
   whose queue is empty (a closed channel is ready). `index` is 0-based. They
   block while no case is ready. The wait is a poll with a 1 ms sleep, not a
   shared wakeup: a channel keeps no list of select waiters, so a select answers
   at most about one poll period after its value arrives. Send cases are not
   in `select`: a send case needs a non-blocking send, which is not built.

   There is no new runtime record. The two ends share one queue and one state
   record through `Mutex.share()` and `Condvar.share()` (a handle to the same
   runtime record, freed when the last handle drops). The queue is a
   `Mutex<List<T>>` that is only ever locked while the `ChanMeta` mutex is
   held, so the meta mutex is the real lock and the queue mutex is the typed
   cell the generic element needs; every wait is a `wait_while` on the meta
   mutex over plain counters, which keeps the predicates non-generic.

   `send` gives the value back as `Some(value)` when the receiver is gone and
   answers `None` when it was queued (so the result is must-use, and `Option`
   is the one payload shape that needs no `unit` value). A bounded send waits
   while the queue is full. `recv` waits while the queue is empty and a sender
   is alive; once the queue is empty and every sender is gone it answers
   `Err(RecvErr.Closed)`. Closing is done by two non-generic `Drop` types
   (`SendEnd`, `RecvEnd`) each end owns: a type parameter cannot carry a
   destructor, so the destructor lives on the part that has none. Their `live`
   field is `false` for the zeroed value a partial move leaves behind, which
   is what lets `val (tx, rx) = sync.bounded(4)` take the pair apart.

   `for v in rx` works because `Receiver<T>` declares `next` on its template;
   the compiler walks any generic `datac` of that shape like an `Iterator`
   (a trait impl cannot name a generic instantiation yet). Everything here is
   hosted only: the module is not in the freestanding admission table, and its
   `Mutex` and `Condvar` are refused there by name. */
impl "std/option"
impl "std/result"
impl "std/thread"

/// Why `recv` has no value: every sender is gone and the queue is empty.
enum RecvErr {
    /// Every sender is gone and the queue is empty.
    Closed
}

/// Why `recv_timeout` has no value.
enum RecvTimeout {
    /// No value arrived in time.
    Timeout
    /// Every sender is gone and the queue is empty.
    Closed
}

/// Everything the two ends agree on, guarded by one mutex. `count` mirrors the
/// queue's length, so the waits never need to look inside the generic queue.
datac ChanMeta(var count: usize, var senders: i64, var rx_alive: bool, val cap: usize, val bounded: bool, val handoff: bool, var pushed: usize, var taken: usize)

/// What a sender owns besides the queue, and the destructor that closes the
/// channel when the last one goes.
datac SendEnd(val live: bool, val meta: Mutex<ChanMeta>, val ne: Condvar, val nf: Condvar)

/// The receiving half's shared state; it closes the channel when dropped.
datac RecvEnd(val live: bool, val meta: Mutex<ChanMeta>, val ne: Condvar, val nf: Condvar)

/// The sending half of a channel.
datac Sender<T>(val q: Mutex<List<T>>, val end: SendEnd)

/// The receiving half of a channel.
datac Receiver<T>(val q: Mutex<List<T>>, val end: RecvEnd)

/// Counts this sender out and wakes a waiting receiver.
fun mut SendEnd.drop() {
    if !live {
        return
    }
    val last = meta.lock { s ->
        s.senders -= 1
        s.senders == 0
    }
    if last {
        ne.notify_all()
    }
}

/// Marks the receiver gone and wakes blocked senders.
fun mut RecvEnd.drop() {
    if !live {
        return
    }
    meta.lock { s -> s.rx_alive = false }
    nf.notify_all()
}

/// One outcome of a `select`: the case at `index` (0-based) received `value`,
/// or the case at `index` is a channel that is closed and empty.
enum Pick<T> {
    /// The case at `index` received a value.
    Got(val index: usize, val value: T)
    /// The case at `index` is closed: every sender is gone and the queue is empty.
    Closed(val index: usize)
}

/// Builds a channel holding at most `cap` values (`bounded`), or none of a limit
/// (`!bounded`). `handoff` is the rendezvous: each send also waits for its take.
fun make_channel<T>(cap: usize, bounded: bool, handoff: bool) => (Sender<T>, Receiver<T>) {
    val meta = Mutex.new(ChanMeta(count = 0, senders = 1, rx_alive = true, cap = cap, bounded = bounded, handoff = handoff, pushed = 0, taken = 0))
    val q: Mutex<List<T>> = Mutex.new(List.empty())
    val ne = Condvar.new()
    val nf = Condvar.new()
    val rx = Receiver<T>(q = q.share(), end = RecvEnd(live = true, meta = meta.share(), ne = ne.share(), nf = nf.share()))
    val tx = Sender<T>(q = q, end = SendEnd(live = true, meta = meta, ne = ne, nf = nf))
    return (tx, rx)
}

/// A channel that holds at most `cap` values: `send` waits while it is full.
fun bounded<T>(cap: usize) => (Sender<T>, Receiver<T>) {
    if cap == 0 {
        panic("sync.bounded: the capacity must be at least 1")
    }
    return make_channel<T>(cap, true, false)
}

/// The zero-capacity channel: `send` returns only once a receive takes its value.
fun rendezvous<T>() => (Sender<T>, Receiver<T>) {
    return make_channel<T>(1, true, true)
}

/// A channel with no limit: `send` never waits.
fun unbounded<T>() => (Sender<T>, Receiver<T>) {
    return make_channel<T>(0, false, false)
}

/// Queue `value`. `None` means it was queued; `Some(value)` hands it back because
/// the receiver is gone. A scoped block cannot capture an owned value by move, so
/// the value waits in a private one-element mutex and the block swaps it across.
/// A rendezvous send also waits until its value is taken, and if the receiver
/// goes away first it takes the value back and hands it over as `Some`.
must_use fun Sender<T>.send(value: move T) => option.Option<T> {
    val staged: Mutex<List<T>> = Mutex.new(List.of(value))
    val r: result.Result<usize, T> = self.end.meta.wait_while(&self.end.nf, fun(s: &ChanMeta) => bool = s.bounded && s.count >= s.cap && s.rx_alive) { s ->
        var mine = staged.swap(List.empty())
        if s.rx_alive {
            s.count += 1
            s.pushed += 1
            var queue = self.q.swap(List.empty())
            queue.push(mine.remove(0))
            val none = self.q.swap(queue)
            val ticket: usize = s.pushed
            val queued: result.Result<usize, T> = result.Result.Ok(value = ticket)
            queued
        } else {
            val back: result.Result<usize, T> = result.Result.Err(error = mine.remove(0))
            back
        }
    }
    self.end.ne.notify_one()
    return when r {
        Err(v) -> {
            val gone: option.Option<T> = option.Option.Some(value = v)
            gone
        }
        Ok(ticket) -> self.await_take(ticket)
    }
}

/// The rendezvous wait: value number `ticket` is taken once `taken` reaches it.
/// Without a handoff the send is already complete and this answers `None`.
fun Sender<T>.await_take(ticket: usize) => option.Option<T> {
    val handoff = self.end.meta.lock { s -> s.handoff }
    if !handoff {
        val none: option.Option<T> = option.Option.None
        return none
    }
    val r = self.end.meta.wait_while(&self.end.nf, fun(s: &ChanMeta) => bool = s.taken < ticket && s.rx_alive) { s ->
        if s.taken >= ticket {
            val taken: option.Option<T> = option.Option.None
            taken
        } else {
            // The receiver is gone and nothing took the value, so it is still
            // the only queued item: take it back for the sender.
            s.count -= 1
            val item = self.q.lock { l -> l.remove(0) }
            val back: option.Option<T> = option.Option.Some(value = item)
            back
        }
    }
    self.end.nf.notify_all()
    return r
}

/// Another sender on the same channel; the channel closes when the last one drops.
fun Sender<T>.clone() => Sender<T> {
    self.end.meta.lock { s -> s.senders += 1 }
    return Sender<T>(q = self.q.share(), end = SendEnd(live = true, meta = self.end.meta.share(), ne = self.end.ne.share(), nf = self.end.nf.share()))
}

/// Take the oldest value, waiting while the queue is empty and a sender is alive.
fun Receiver<T>.recv() => result.Result<T, RecvErr> {
    val r = self.end.meta.wait_while(&self.end.ne, fun(s: &ChanMeta) => bool = s.count == 0 && s.senders > 0) { s ->
        if s.count > 0 {
            s.count -= 1
            s.taken += 1
            val item = self.q.lock { l -> l.remove(0) }
            val got: result.Result<T, RecvErr> = result.Result.Ok(value = item)
            got
        } else {
            val closed: result.Result<T, RecvErr> = result.Result.Err(error = RecvErr.Closed)
            closed
        }
    }
    self.end.nf.notify_all()
    return r
}

/// The oldest value if there is one now; never waits.
fun Receiver<T>.try_recv() => option.Option<T> {
    val r = self.end.meta.lock { s ->
        if s.count > 0 {
            s.count -= 1
            s.taken += 1
            val item = self.q.lock { l -> l.remove(0) }
            option.Option.Some(value = item)
        } else {
            val none: option.Option<T> = option.Option.None
            none
        }
    }
    self.end.nf.notify_all()
    return r
}

/// Like `recv`, but gives up after `millis` milliseconds with `Err(Timeout)`.
fun Receiver<T>.recv_timeout(millis: i64) => result.Result<T, RecvTimeout> {
    val r = self.end.meta.wait_while_timeout(&self.end.ne, fun(s: &ChanMeta) => bool = s.count == 0 && s.senders > 0, millis) { s ->
        if s.count > 0 {
            s.count -= 1
            s.taken += 1
            val item = self.q.lock { l -> l.remove(0) }
            val got: result.Result<T, RecvTimeout> = result.Result.Ok(value = item)
            got
        } else {
            val closed: result.Result<T, RecvTimeout> = result.Result.Err(error = RecvTimeout.Closed)
            closed
        }
    }
    self.end.nf.notify_all()
    return when r {
        Some(v) -> v
        None -> {
            val late: result.Result<T, RecvTimeout> = result.Result.Err(error = RecvTimeout.Timeout)
            late
        }
    }
}

/// `for v in rx`: one value per pass until every sender is gone.
fun mut Receiver<T>.next() => option.Option<T> {
    return when self.recv() {
        Ok(v) -> option.Option.Some(value = v)
        Err(_) -> {
            val done: option.Option<T> = option.Option.None
            done
        }
    }
}

/// One non-blocking look at a receive case: `None` while the channel is open and
/// empty, `Some(Ok(v))` for a value taken now, `Some(Err(Closed))` once it is
/// closed and empty. Used by `select2` and `select3`, which poll it.
fun Receiver<T>.poll() => option.Option<result.Result<T, RecvErr>> {
    val r = self.end.meta.lock { s ->
        if s.count > 0 {
            s.count -= 1
            s.taken += 1
            val item = self.q.lock { l -> l.remove(0) }
            val got: result.Result<T, RecvErr> = result.Result.Ok(value = item)
            val ready: option.Option<result.Result<T, RecvErr>> = option.Option.Some(value = got)
            ready
        } else if s.senders == 0 {
            val gone: result.Result<T, RecvErr> = result.Result.Err(error = RecvErr.Closed)
            val closed: option.Option<result.Result<T, RecvErr>> = option.Option.Some(value = gone)
            closed
        } else {
            val none: option.Option<result.Result<T, RecvErr>> = option.Option.None
            none
        }
    }
    self.end.nf.notify_all()
    return r
}

/// The `Pick` for case `index` once its poll has answered.
fun pick_of<T>(index: usize, r: move result.Result<T, RecvErr>) => Pick<T> {
    return when r {
        Ok(v) -> Pick.Got(index = index, value = v)
        Err(_) -> Pick.Closed(index = index)
    }
}

/// Waits for whichever of two receive cases is ready first; the lower index wins a tie.
fun select2<T>(a: &Receiver<T>, b: &Receiver<T>) => Pick<T> {
    while true {
        when a.poll() {
            Some(r) -> {
                return pick_of(0, r)
            }
            None -> {}
        }
        when b.poll() {
            Some(r) -> {
                return pick_of(1, r)
            }
            None -> {}
        }
        thread.sleep_ms(1)
    }
    panic("select2: unreachable")
}

/// Waits for whichever of three receive cases is ready first; the lowest index wins a tie.
fun select3<T>(a: &Receiver<T>, b: &Receiver<T>, c: &Receiver<T>) => Pick<T> {
    while true {
        when a.poll() {
            Some(r) -> {
                return pick_of(0, r)
            }
            None -> {}
        }
        when b.poll() {
            Some(r) -> {
                return pick_of(1, r)
            }
            None -> {}
        }
        when c.poll() {
            Some(r) -> {
                return pick_of(2, r)
            }
            None -> {}
        }
        thread.sleep_ms(1)
    }
    panic("select3: unreachable")
}
